part.text:
I confirmed the API. In tRPC v11, `useSuspenseInfiniteQuery` returns a tuple `[data, result]` where:
- `data` is the `InfiniteData` (with `.pages`)
- `result` is the query result object with `fetchNextPage`, `hasNextPage`, `isFetchingNextPage`, etc.

Looking at the source:
```js
function useSuspenseInfiniteQuery(path, input, opts) {
    ...
    return [hook.data!, hook as any];
}
```

And the type:
```ts
export type useTRPCSuspenseInfiniteQuery<TDef extends ResolverDef> = (
  input: InfiniteInput<TDef['input']>,
  opts: makeInfiniteQueryOptions<...>,
) => [
  trpcInfiniteData<TDef>,
  TRPCHookResult & UseSuspenseInfiniteQueryResult<...>,
];
```

So my PostList.tsx is correct: `const [data, query] = trpc.posts.list.useSuspenseInfiniteQuery(...)`.

But wait, I destructured as `const [data] = ...` and then used `data.pages`, `data.hasNextPage`, `data.fetchNextPage`, `data.isFetchingNextPage`. That's wrong! `hasNextPage`, `fetchNextPage`, `isFetchingNextPage` are on the second element (the query result), not on `data`.

Let me fix the PostList.tsx to destructure both elements.

input.content:
'use client';

import { trpc } from '../../trpc/client';
import React from 'react';

export function PostList() {
  const [data, query] = trpc.posts.list.useSuspenseInfiniteQuery(
    {},
    {
      getNextPageParam: (lastPage) => lastPage.nextCursor,
      initialCursor: 0,
    }
  );

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Posts</h2>
      <ul>
        {data.pages.map((page) =>
          page.items.map((post) => (
            <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
          ))
        )}
      </ul>
      <button
        disabled={!query.hasNextPage || query.isFetchingNextPage}
        onClick={() => query.fetchNextPage()}
      >
        Load More
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

